# Article Writing & Document Types

## Frontmatter (REQUIRED)

Every `.md` article MUST start with YAML frontmatter:

```yaml
---
title: Human-Readable Title
description: One-line SEO description (50-160 characters)
---
```

## Content Quality Standards

> **Two non-negotiable principles that must be balanced:**
> 1. **Complete** — every important concept, rule, workflow step, and validation must be present
> 2. **Direct** — explained in the fewest words possible, straight to the point

### Writing must be
- **Complete AND concise.** Never omit important details to hit a word target — shorten the wording instead.
- **Plain language.** Write as if explaining to a first-time user, not a developer.
- **Actionable.** Tell users what to click, what to expect, what it means.
- **Direct.** Lead with the answer; skip background. No "it is important to understand…"

### What to REMOVE from every article
- Backend field names, API paths, internal status codes (unless shown to users on screen)
- System internals (webhooks, middleware, Snowflake, blob storage, rate limiting, code refactoring)
- Filler sentences: "This section explains…", "It is important to note…"
- "Impact on Existing Users" sections
- Long paragraph introductions — one clear sentence is enough

### What to KEEP (never remove)
- Every business rule, validation constraint, and workflow step that affects the user
- Button names, field labels, status names exactly as shown on screen
- Numbered steps — one line per step, but cover ALL steps completely
- Status tables with all statuses and their meaning
- Cross-reference links to related articles (instead of repeating content)
- Screenshots placed immediately below the step or section they illustrate

### Word targets (guidelines, not hard limits — completeness always wins)
- Process articles (Type D, L): aim for 400–600 words
- Concept / Overview articles (Type J): aim for 300–500 words
- Status / Reference articles (Type E, G): aim for 250–400 words
- FAQ answers: 2–5 sentences per answer, then a back-link
- If an article cannot be complete under the target, keep the content and review the structure

---

## Screenshot Rules (MANDATORY)

### Placement
- Place screenshots **immediately below** the step or explanation they illustrate
- Never group all screenshots at the end of an article

### Layout
- **Default: one screenshot per line** with a caption directly below it
- **Maximum two screenshots side-by-side** (only when directly comparing two views)
- Never place 3+ screenshots in one row
- Every screenshot must have a caption describing what it shows

### Format
```markdown
![Caption describing the screenshot](images/NN-article-name/filename.png)
*Caption describing the screenshot*
```

---

## No Information Repetition Rule

- **Never repeat the same information in two articles.**
- If another article explains something in detail, give a one-line summary and link: `→ See [Article Title](filename.md) for details.`
- Only repeat information if it is essential for the user to act without navigating away (e.g., a critical warning).

---

## Section Flow Order Rule

Articles within each sidebar section **must follow the platform's workflow order** — the sequence a user encounters features in practice.

- **Asset Sale order:** Overview → Analytics → Deal Lifecycle → Deal Creation → Investor Commitment → Investor Agreement & E-Signature → Settlement → Repayment → NFT Burn
- **Credit Facilities order:** Overview → Roles → Term Sheet → Master Commitment → Facility Setup → Funding Requests → Funding Notices → Token/E-Sign
- Any new section must be inserted at the position that matches when the user encounters it in the workflow.

